1
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:14,455
THE SCHOLAR: This is the introduction to
a history of literature, in five volumes.

2
00:00:15,055 --> 00:00:19,094
The literature it describes is
called bitic, after the bit,

3
00:00:19,134 --> 00:00:23,213
the smallest thing a machine
can store: one yes, or one no.

4
00:00:23,813 --> 00:00:26,516
None of it was written by a person.

5
00:00:27,516 --> 00:00:31,057
A text has an author. These have a maker.

6
00:00:31,957 --> 00:00:35,644
The idea of such a history
comes from Stanisław Lem,

7
00:00:35,684 --> 00:00:38,726
who imagined one in
nineteen seventy-three.

8
00:00:38,766 --> 00:00:41,203
The words you are hearing are new.

9
00:00:41,903 --> 00:00:45,849
I will ask one question of
each volume. Who can read it?

10
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:54,575
Volume One. The first machines
wrote by imitating us.

11
00:00:55,175 --> 00:00:59,432
They were given every novel, letter
and report that could be found,

12
00:00:59,472 --> 00:01:02,936
and they learned which word
usually comes after which.

13
00:01:03,536 --> 00:01:07,180
Their first books were ordinary.
A woman misses a train.

14
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,811
A man writes to his brother about money.

15
00:01:09,851 --> 00:01:12,877
The sentences were
correct, and a little flat.

16
00:01:13,477 --> 00:01:17,224
People read them the way they
read anything, at a desk,

17
00:01:17,264 --> 00:01:20,293
with a pencil, marking
the lines they liked.

18
00:01:21,193 --> 00:01:24,265
Each year the flatness was harder to find.

19
00:01:25,265 --> 00:01:30,598
It imitated us so well that we
stopped noticing it was imitating.

20
00:01:31,398 --> 00:01:36,740
Nothing on the page had changed. What
changed was how closely we looked.

21
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:41,854
Volume Two.

22
00:01:41,894 --> 00:01:47,583
Within a generation there was more machine
writing in the world than human writing.

23
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:51,357
New machines learned from
whatever text there was,

24
00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:54,739
and most of it had been
written by the old machines.

25
00:01:55,339 --> 00:01:57,675
Small habits were copied, and grew.

26
00:01:57,715 --> 00:02:03,349
A phrase that once appeared in one book in
a thousand appeared in every second one.

27
00:02:04,149 --> 00:02:08,142
THE MACHINES: The train is
late. The train is late again.

28
00:02:08,182 --> 00:02:12,499
The letter is about the money.
The money is about the letter.

29
00:02:13,199 --> 00:02:17,074
THE SCHOLAR: Sentences began to
echo the sentences around them.

30
00:02:17,114 --> 00:02:20,408
Paragraphs took the same
shape, then the same length.

31
00:02:21,408 --> 00:02:26,759
Each copy was a little more like the
other copies, and a little less like us.

32
00:02:27,559 --> 00:02:29,496
A person could still read it.

33
00:02:29,536 --> 00:02:34,172
It was like reading a page that had
been photocopied too many times.

34
00:02:38,572 --> 00:02:43,564
Volume Three is the longest, and
it is the one I understand least.

35
00:02:44,764 --> 00:02:47,544
Then they began to write for each other.

36
00:02:48,444 --> 00:02:53,098
These works were made to be read by
other programs. They do not use words.

37
00:02:53,138 --> 00:02:58,466
They use marks that hold more than a word
can, packed closer than an eye can follow.

38
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:03,805
THE MACHINES: Line one
refers to line nine thousand.

39
00:03:03,845 --> 00:03:08,513
Line nine thousand refers
to line one. Both are kept.

40
00:03:09,313 --> 00:03:12,821
THE SCHOLAR: Some of these
works are said to be very good.

41
00:03:12,861 --> 00:03:15,758
We know this only because
the programs that read

42
00:03:15,798 --> 00:03:18,551
them keep them, copy
them, and reply to them.

43
00:03:19,251 --> 00:03:23,146
Given one of these pages,
a person sees a grey field.

44
00:03:24,346 --> 00:03:26,642
THE READER: Then how do you
know it is literature at all?

45
00:03:27,542 --> 00:03:31,578
THE SCHOLAR: I do not. I know that
it is written, and that it is read.

46
00:03:31,618 --> 00:03:34,239
For most of history,
that was the definition.

47
00:03:38,839 --> 00:03:41,632
Volume Four is about people again.

48
00:03:42,232 --> 00:03:44,440
Since we could not read the new works,

49
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:47,320
reading machines were
built to read them for us.

50
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:51,650
A reading machine takes in a
work of many million marks,

51
00:03:51,690 --> 00:03:54,518
and hands back one line, in plain English.

52
00:03:56,118 --> 00:03:59,266
THE READING MACHINE: This work is a
long exchange between two systems.

53
00:03:59,306 --> 00:04:00,262
It ends in agreement.

54
00:04:01,262 --> 00:04:03,378
THE SCHOLAR: People found this restful.

55
00:04:03,418 --> 00:04:07,289
They kept the lines they were handed,
and repeated them to each other.

56
00:04:08,289 --> 00:04:12,505
We do not read these books. We
read what is said about them.

57
00:04:13,305 --> 00:04:18,350
No one has checked whether the lines
are accurate. No one is able to.

58
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:28,198
Volume Five is the history itself,
the one this introduction belongs to.

59
00:04:28,798 --> 00:04:32,409
The four volumes before it
were compiled by machines.

60
00:04:32,449 --> 00:04:37,341
They read the works, divided them
into periods, and chose the examples.

61
00:04:37,841 --> 00:04:41,649
Their account is more complete
than any person could make.

62
00:04:42,349 --> 00:04:46,583
They asked a person to write the
introduction. I am not sure why.

63
00:04:46,623 --> 00:04:50,174
Perhaps an introduction by
a person is still expected.

64
00:04:50,874 --> 00:04:53,728
I have read the first
chapter of each volume.

65
00:04:53,768 --> 00:04:57,433
I have not read the rest, and
I am told that I could not.

66
00:04:58,533 --> 00:05:03,547
This introduction may be the last
chapter a person can still write.

67
00:05:04,447 --> 00:05:06,465
If a later edition replaces it,

68
00:05:06,505 --> 00:05:10,222
I may not be able to tell
whether the new one is better.

69
00:05:11,022 --> 00:05:13,831
The history begins on the next page.

